          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#syntax"} Syntax
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#judgmental_structure"} Judgmental Structure
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#type_structure"} Type Structure
                %ul
                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#shifts"} Shifts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#as_an_adjoint_logic"} As an Adjoint Logic
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related"} Related
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Call-by-push-value (CBPV) is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/programming+language"} programming language
            paradigm which has full embeddings of
            %span.newWikiWord
              call-by-value
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/call-by-value"} ?
            and
            %span.newWikiWord
              call-by-name
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/call-by-name"} ?
            lambda-calculi, including their
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operational+semantics"} operational semantics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equational+theories"} equational theories
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/denotational+semantics"} denotational
            models.
          %p
            Its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semantics"} semantics
            decomposes the semantics of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad+%28in+computer+science%29"} effectful languages
            using a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+monad"} strong monad
            into a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+adjunction"} strong adjunction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+category"} Kleisli and co-Kleisli categories
            from an adjunction.
          %h2#syntax Syntax
          %h3#judgmental_structure Judgmental Structure
          %p
            CBPV has two “kinds” of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,A',A_i
            — and computation types — written
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B,B',B_i
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgments"} judgments
            of CBPV: Values, Terms, and Stacks.
          %p
            The value judgment is an ordinary simple type theory. A context
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            is a sequence of value typed variables
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1:A_1,\ldots
            and the judgment
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi V
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash V : A
            admits symmetry, contraction, weakening and the corresponding substitution principle: if for every
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_i:A_i
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma' \vdash \gamma(x_i) : A_i
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma' \vdash V[\gamma] : A
          %p
            The term judgment has again a value typed context
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi M
                    %mo :
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash M : B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma
            as above, then there is a term
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo :
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma' \vdash M[\gamma] : B
          %p
            Finally the stack judgment has a value context
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            as input, but also a computation type as input and output:
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi B
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi S
                    %mo :
                    %mi B
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma | B \vdash S : B'
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S[\gamma]
            as above. Stacks also have a composition operation: if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi S
                  %mo :
                  %mi B
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma | B \vdash S : B'
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi B
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi S
                  %mo ′
                  %mo :
                  %mi B
                  %mo ″
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma | B' \vdash S' : B''
            then
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi B
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi S
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo :
                    %mi B
                    %mo ″
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma | B \vdash S'[S] : B''
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi B
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mo •
                    %mo :
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma | B \vdash \bullet : B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi M
                  %mo :
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash M : B
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi S
                  %mo :
                  %mi B
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma | B \vdash S : B'
            then
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo :
                    %mi B
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash S[M] : B'
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bullet
            as identity.
          %p
            We can get a slightly simpler presentation by combining the stack and term judgments into a single term judgment with a
            = succeed ":" do
              %span.newWikiWord
                stoup
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/stoup"} ?
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            or empty. Then a term as before is a term typed with the empty stoup and a stack is a term typed with a full stoup. This has the benefit of combining the action of stacks on terms and the composition of stacks as one operation. Below we write a stoup as
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
          %h3#type_structure Type Structure
          %p
            Call-by-push-value is characterized not just by its judgmental structure, but by a specific
            %em choice
            of which connectives to use. Except for the shifts
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo ,
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F,U
          %p
            For this reason, some connectives are definable from the judgments but excluded because they violate this discipline. These are a tensor product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \otimes B
            which is a computation type, a unit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            computation type, and a linear function space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊸
                  %mi B
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \multimap B'
            which is a computation type. When these are added, the system is called the
            %a{:href => "#EEC"} Enriched Effect Calculus
            (modulo some superficial syntactic differences).
          %h4#shifts Shifts
          %p
            The shift types express the adjunction between values and stacks. The type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi UB
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} UB
            is a value type that is pronounced as “thunk of
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closures"} closures
            that when called behave as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi M
                      %mo :
                      %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi thunk
                      %mi M
                      %mo :
                      %mi UB
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac { \Gamma \vdash M : B }
                  { \Gamma \vdash thunk M : UB }
          %p and elimination rule:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi V
                      %mo :
                      %mi UB
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi force
                      %mi V
                      %mo :
                      %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac { \Gamma \vdash V : UB }
                  { \Gamma \vdash force V : B }
          %p
            With
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi β
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta
            rule
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi force
                  %mi thunk
                  %mi M
                  %mo =
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} force thunk M = M
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
            rule
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo =
                    %mi thunk
                    %mi force
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V = thunk force V
          %p
            The type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi FA
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FA
            is a computation type that is the type of “returners of
            = succeed "s\u201D." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi M
                      %mo :
                      %mi F
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi N
                      %mo :
                      %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi M
                      %mi to
                      %mi x
                      %mo .
                      %mi N
                      %mo :
                      %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac { \Gamma | \Delta \vdash M : F A \qquad \Gamma, x : A \vdash N : B}
                  { \Gamma | \Delta \vdash M to x. N : B }
          %p with introduction rule:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi V
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi return
                      %mi V
                      %mo :
                      %mi F
                      %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac {\Gamma \vdash V : A } { \Gamma \vdash return V : F A }
          %p
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi β
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta
            rule
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi return
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi to
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                  %mi N
                  %mo =
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (return V) to x. N = N[V/x]
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
            rule that for any stack
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi F
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi S
                    %mo :
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma | F A \vdash S : B
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo =
                    %mo •
                    %mi to
                    %mi x
                    %mo .
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi return
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S = \bullet to x. S[return x]
          %h2#as_an_adjoint_logic As an Adjoint Logic
          %p
            The semantics of Call-by-push-value was originally presented using fibred categories, but we can get a presentation of its semantics in a more multi-categorical flavor by expressing it as an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+logic"} adjoint logic
            from a specific mode theory in the sense of
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#LSR"} LSR
          %p
            As a summary, the mode theory of CBPV is the theory of a cartesian monoid acting on a pointed object. In more detail, we have two modes:
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
            for values and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            for computations. Then the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
            mode is axiomatized as a cartesian monoid
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi v
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ×
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (v,\times,1)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo :
                  %mi v
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo :
                  %mi c
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi a
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi b
                  %mo :
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a:v,b:c \vdash a\otimes b : c
          %p
            to represent the combined context
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma | B
            and also a “point” for the computation types:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi i
                  %mo :
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vdash i : c
          %p which represents the empty stoup.
          %p To correctly model the substitution structure, we add equations that say that the tensor product constitutes an action of the monoid, i.e., associativity and unitality:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ×
                  %mi a
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a \times a') \otimes b = a \otimes (a' \otimes b) \qquad 1 \otimes b = b
          %p
            Then for example, the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            type above is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_{x \otimes i}
            and the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            type is
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo .
                        %mi x
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_{x. x \otimes i}
          %p
            Furthermore, the mode theory also gives the “missing” types of the Enriched Effect Calculus. The tensor product is
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_{x \otimes y}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_{i}
            and the linear function space is
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo .
                        %mi x
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_{x. x\otimes y}
          %p
            Note that this mode theory is equivalent to the one given for a strong monad in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#LSR"} LSR
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            adds a morphism
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo :
                    %mi v
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a : v \vdash g(a) : c
            = succeed "-actions" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ×
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g(a \times b) = a \otimes g(b)
          %p
            However, this is equivalent to our above presentation because for any
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo :
                    %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a:v
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                    %mo ×
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a = a \times 1
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ×
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g(a) = g(a \times 1) = a\otimes g(1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            is completely determined by where it takes
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
          %h2#related Related
          %ul
            %li
              The linear term judgment in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear-non-linear+logic"} linear-non-linear logic
              can be seen as extending the call-by-push-value stack judgment to allow for “multi-hole” stacks.
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul-Blain+Levy"}> Paul-Blain Levy
                ,
                %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/3-540-48959-2_17"}
                  %em> Call-by-Push-Value: A Subsuming Paradigm
                  , TLCA 1999
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul-Blain+Levy"}> Paul-Blain Levy
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Adjunction Models for Call-by-push-value with Stacks
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.cs.bham.ac.uk/~pbl/papers/ctcs02journal.pdf"} CTCS version
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1571066104805681"} EPTCS journal version
            %li#EEC
              %p
                Jeff Egger, Rasmus Ejlers Møgelberg and Alex Simpson,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://pdfs.semanticscholar.org/2f3f/21259ffaa5cc56bb280914a028cfed3454e2.pdf"} The Enriched Effect Calculus: Syntax and Semantics
            %li#LSR
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Licata"}> Daniel Licata
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"} Mike Shulman
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mitchell+Riley"} Mitchell Riley
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A Fibrational Framework for Substructural and Modal Logics (extended version)
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://drops.dagstuhl.de/opus/volltexte/2017/7740/"} doi: 10.4230/LIPIcs.FSCD.2017.25
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://dlicata.web.wesleyan.edu/pubs/lsr17multi/lsr17multi-ex.pdf"} pdf
